WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:04.170
We want to expand the conversation now
with someone who is there on January six.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.173
CNN law enforcement
analyst Michael Fanone is a former D.C.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.343
Metro police officer
who was attacked by that mob

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:12.846
and was injured responding to the attack.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.549
Michael, thank you so much for sharing
part of your afternoon with us.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.520
Your response
initially to the sentencing of Stuart

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.555
Rhodes, the head of the Oath Keepers,

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:29.496
I certainly think that the guilty verdict

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.633
and a significant sentence
like 18 years is appropriate

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.003
although I would have agreed more with the

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:41.074
the government's recommendation
of 25 years.

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:44.611
And Michael, I'm curious.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:47.580
We heard from his attorneys
they repeatedly kept saying that

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.618
the Oath Keepers meant to deter violence
that day.

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:55.188
Obviously we're watching video of the mob
targeting

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.058
law enforcement officers just like you.

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:01.294
I'm wondering
how you respond to his attorneys saying

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:04.397
that he's being convicted for his words.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:06.766
He's being sentenced for his
words and not his actions.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:10.403
Yeah.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:13.106
I mean, that's something that I would
expect the defense attorney to say.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.042
But the facts

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.379
spell out a very different story

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:23.283
as to Stewart Rhodes and the organization,
which he led,

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.554
participation in the violence

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:29.823
that was January 6th.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.726
This is a group that came to the capital

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.998
prepared, that made preparation
in advance of January 6th.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.135
They trained they stockpiled weapons

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:45.705
and ammunition
in preparation for that day.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:49.142
There was a great deal of organization
and effort

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.112
facilitated by Stewart Rhodes

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:55.048
as the leader of the The Oath Keepers.

00:01:56.216 --> 00:02:00.453
And obviously, we saw the violence
that played out that day.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:03.056
And all of this was in an effort

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.427
to overturn the results of a free and fair
election.

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:11.798
And election that had its challenges

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:17.837
brought through the court system
60 plus times and each time found to be

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:22.675
upheld and legitimate.

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.377
And yet, which is why we see

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:27.013
the campaign for seditious conspiracy.

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:28.615
Right. Absolutely.

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:32.585
And yet we see so many of the former
president's cronies

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:36.956
and the former president himself
repeat the lie that there was widespread

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.258
fraud in the last election.

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:40.160
And on that note.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.429
We spoke to an attorney, legal expert
in the previous hour

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:47.834
who said that part of the reason
for Stewart's road strategy of remaining

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:51.804
unrepentant through all of this
legal proceeding is because he believes

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:56.042
he may receive a pardon
if the former president is reelected.

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:59.979
I'm wondering what you make of Stewart
Rhodes potentially

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.882
being pardoned down the road
before serving a full sentence,

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:08.655
despite the obvious harm that the court
that the court ruled that he caused

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:13.593
I mean, it's outrageous.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:16.396
It it should outrage all Americans.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.732
You know, Stewart
Rhodes was convicted by a jury

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:22.302
of his peers for his actions,

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.471
which resulted in

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:29.842
the injuries sustained by myself
and hundreds of other police officers.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:32.111
On January six.

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:35.181
Not to mention the fact
that America became

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:38.117
a national and international embarrassment

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.954
on the world stage

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:44.891
But I think that
this is part of a strategy,

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:48.761
not just a strategy on behalf of January
six defendants

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:52.465
like Stewart Rhodes, but also a strategy
on behalf of the former president.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:54.701
You know, if the former president

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:57.837
was to admit that it was, in fact,
a free and fair election,

00:03:58.538 --> 00:04:01.741
then he would also, in turn be admitting

00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:06.846
some degree of culpability
for the violence that took place that day.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.717
January six.

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:13.553
Stewart Rhodes, I think, is

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:17.624
he is looking for a

00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:18.458
pardon.

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:21.928
It's sickening
that that the former president

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:24.998
would have aligned himself
with individuals like this

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:28.901
in the statements that he made
in this town hall and continues

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.204
to make on a daily basis

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:37.176
Officer Michael Fanone,
we appreciate your service on that day

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:40.546
as we watch video of the ugliness
that unfolded.

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:43.383
And we appreciate you speaking out
since then.

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:45.251
Thank you so much for the time

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:47.854
Thanks for having me.

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:50.723
Of course, the leader of the Oath Keepers

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:53.960
has just been sentenced for his actions
in the attack on the U.S.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.928
Capitol on January 6th.

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.830
Stuart
Rhodes, the founder and leader of the far

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.867
right group, was the first of nine
defendants who were sentenced.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.438
The judge finding that his actions
amounted to domestic terrorism

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:08.574
and for that,
he will be serving 18 years in prison.

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:12.612
We have CNN's senior crime and justice
reporter Caitlin Lance joining us now.

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:14.947
Caitlin, walk us through
what happened today.

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:16.049
Inside of court

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:22.889
Well, Brianna, this was a very intense
sentencing hearing

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:26.826
today, one where
student Stuart Rhodes expressed no remorse

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:30.797
for what he did on January six
and continued to espouse his belief

00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:35.401
that the election of 2020 was illegal
and that he was a political prisoner.

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:38.871
But before the judge gave him
his sentence, he was sentenced

00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:42.875
to 18 years in prison
and also some years of supervised release.

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.177
After that,

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:48.448
the judge was very straightforward
with Stuart Rhodes about what he did,

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:51.417
why seditious conspiracy
was so significant

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:54.420
of a crime, one that strikes
at the heart of American democracy.

00:05:54.721 --> 00:05:57.924
He said to Stuart Rhodes,
This is the judge I made that

00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:00.460
you are not a political prisoner,
Mr. Rhodes.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.328
That is not why you are here.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.163
It is not because of your beliefs.

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:07.500
It is not because Joe
Biden is the president right now.

00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:09.635
The judge had many things to say to them.

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:14.173
Some of the other things he said
was that he believes that Rhodes is making

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:18.478
clear today in court when he spoke
to the judge expressing no remorse

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:22.281
that violence could be something
that achieved his goals

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:24.851
and that he continued to be a threat
to democracy.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:30.623
The judge clearly took that to heart
and was saying that we cannot have this.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:31.891
We cannot have citizens

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:35.895
believe that violence is the way
to get things done in a democracy.

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:41.167
And the judge also expressed some concern
of whether there would be another January

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:45.938
six, that any elections in the future,
they will there will be fear around them.

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:48.408
Because of what happened on January six.

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:52.078
But 18 years is quite
a significant sentence by far, the longest

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:55.281
that any January six riot
defendant has received

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:57.850
for federal crimes on that day.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:02.355
And by my calculation, it puts Stuart
Rhodes in jail somewhere near

00:07:02.355 --> 00:07:07.794
or after 2040, which is five
presidential elections from now Brianna.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:10.363
Wow. Caitlin Plants, thank you.

00:07:10.363 --> 00:07:12.298
I want to bring in Evan Perez now.

00:07:12.298 --> 00:07:17.036
Evan, let's just be clear about
how we got to this point, what Stuart

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:22.408
Rhodes did leading up to January 6th
and even after January 6th.

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:24.844
Right.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:28.815
That's part of the key evidence that
the prosecutors presented at this trial.

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:33.586
They said
and they showed evidence of the amount

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:36.856
of coordination that they say Stuart
Rhodes and some of these other members

00:07:37.356 --> 00:07:39.425
went through before January six.

00:07:39.425 --> 00:07:43.396
There was efforts to do some training,
military style training,

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:45.731
militia style
training in North Carolina, for instance.

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:50.136
So they stockpiled weapons in a van
in Northern Virginia

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:54.540
with the idea that, you know, if
and when the former president, President

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:58.578
Trump at the time were to declare
and invoke the Insurrection

00:07:58.578 --> 00:08:01.347
Act, that they could be the foot soldiers,
they would be the militia

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:06.219
that would cross over into Washington
and help him keep power.

00:08:06.786 --> 00:08:09.989
Even after the dust has settled on January

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:13.759
six, even after it was clear
that Donald Trump would be leaving.

00:08:14.026 --> 00:08:15.828
They vowed to keep fighting on.

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:20.132
They vowed to keep to prevent the former
the new president,

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:23.836
their lawfully elected president,
Joe Biden, from being able to take office.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:28.875
So those are the things that prosecutors
were successful in playing

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:31.978
for the jurors, showing them photographs,
showing them some of the messages

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:36.949
that were being exchanged that persuaded
that jury to come back with the

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:40.119
with the extraordinary thing,
which is to convict these people

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:45.391
on seditious conspiracy, essentially
trying to overthrow the US government.

00:08:45.725 --> 00:08:49.495
And why you see this very stiff
sentence today from this judge.

00:08:49.729 --> 00:08:55.368
18 years, the longest that has been given
to anybody in these in these cases

00:08:55.368 --> 00:08:59.438
really the general six yet saying
that it amounts to domestic terrorism.

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:01.574
Really
something to hear the judge say that.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:03.476
Evan Perez, Caitlin Lance, thank you.

